## <span style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Using Health Technology to Form Good Habits – Lesson # 1</span>
<span style="font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</span>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Checking in on Health</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Using Health Technology to Form Good Habits</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of extended discussions on abstract topics. Interactive: Simulate negotiation scenarios in various settings.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate understanding of extended discussions about health technologies and long-term habits (e.g., "I have been using a fitness app to improve my workout routine.")<br>Can identify key points in conversations about mental health practices (e.g., "You might need to practice mindfulness to reduce stress.")<br>Can follow a simulated negotiation between a patient and a health coach about creating a fitness plan (e.g., "I can exercise three times a week, but I prefer yoga over running.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and pronunciation for the listening task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various health technologies (e.g., fitness trackers, meditation apps, hydration reminder apps). Students quickly discuss in pairs what each technology is for and if they or someone they know has been using them. Teacher introduces core vocabulary: <i>tracker, app, habit, well-being, mindfulness, hydration, balance, improve, monitor</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary, focusing on the voiced /b/ in "balance" and "boost," contrasting it with the voiceless /p/ in "practice" and "prevent." The teacher also models the voiceless /s/ in "stress" and "sleep," compared to the voiced /z/ in "exercise." Teacher demonstrates active listening strategies like listening for main ideas and specific details. Teacher models sentence rhythm for the present perfect continuous (e.g., "I have been drinking more water"), emphasizing content words like "drinking" and reducing auxiliary verbs like "have" (/həv/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you have a 'habit' of drinking coffee, is that something you do once or regularly?" (Regularly)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does a 'fitness tracker' do? Does it tell you what to eat or how many steps you've taken?" (How many steps you've taken / activity level)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When someone says they are 'practicing mindfulness,' are they usually very stressed or trying to feel calm?" (Trying to feel calm, manage stress)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Checking in on Health."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio dialogue (approx. 2-3 minutes) between two high school students or young adults discussing their experiences with health technology and forming good habits. The dialogue incorporates target vocabulary (<i>health, fitness, well-being, hydration, mindfulness, balance, habits, stress, tracker, app, routine, energy, nutrition, lifestyle, self-care, monitor, improve, recommend</i>) and grammar (present perfect continuous, second conditional, modals for possibility). Register is semi-formal to informal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understands extended speech and lectures and follows even complex lines of argument provided the topic is reasonably familiar and the direction of the talk is clearly signposted.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen once and identify the main topic of the conversation. (e.g., "What health habits and technologies are they primarily discussing?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and answer True/False questions based on general details.</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One speaker has been using a hydration app to drink more water. (T)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. The other speaker thinks fitness trackers are a waste of money. (F)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. They discuss how health technology has improved their overall well-being. (T)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Information Matching):</b> Students match specific health technologies mentioned to the benefit each speaker has experienced.</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A. Mindfulness app &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 1. Better sleep patterns</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">B. Fitness tracker &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 2. Reduced stress levels</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">C. Hydration app &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 3. More consistent activity levels</li>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, preparing students to recognize and understand target language in context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present perfect continuous (ongoing efforts), second conditional (hypothetical well-being), modals for possibility (health strategies).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> <i>health, fitness, well-being, hydration, mindfulness, balance, habits, stress, tracker, app, routine, improve, track, support, boost, recommend, mindful, consistent, should, might, could, can.</i></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /b/ vs /p/, /s/ vs /z/, sentence rhythm in present perfect continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Identifying health suggestions ("You might want to try..."), expressing empathy ("That sounds tough.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Recognizing polite and respectful tones in suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listening for Grammar):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the audio, but with blanks for the target grammar structures. They listen to specific segments of the audio again, focusing on completing the sentences with the exact present perfect continuous verbs or modal phrases they hear.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "I ____ ____ (exercise) daily to improve my fitness." (have been exercising)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "If I ____ (have) more free time, I ____ (join) a yoga class." (had, would join)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "You ____ ____ (try) mindfulness exercises to reduce stress." (might need to try / could try)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Listening for Vocabulary & Pronunciation):</b> The teacher plays 5-6 short, targeted phrases or sentences from the audio that highlight the new vocabulary and contrasting sounds (/b/-/p/, /s/-/z/). Students listen and circle the correct word from a choice of two similar-sounding words on their worksheet, or fill in a blank. The teacher then replays for students to repeat, focusing on accurate pronunciation and sentence rhythm.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "My fitness tracker helps me <b>(balance / boost)</b> my activity level." (boost)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "I've been feeling a lot of <b>(stress / sleep)</b> lately." (stress)</li>
                    </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Listening Check):</b> Students are given a list of 5-7 quotes or paraphrased statements from the audio. In pairs, they listen to the audio one final time (or specific segments) and categorize each statement based on its pragmatic function (e.g., "Giving a health suggestion," "Expressing an ongoing habit," "Discussing a hypothetical situation," "Expressing empathy"). They compare their categorizations and justify their choices based on what they heard.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Checking in on Health." Focus on demonstrating listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format (Simulated Negotiation Listening):</b> Students will listen to an extended audio recording (approx. 3-4 minutes) of a simulated negotiation between a patient and a health coach about creating a personalized fitness and well-being plan, using health technology. The negotiation includes suggestions, counter-offers, and agreements, making use of second conditionals, present perfect continuous, and modals for possibility, alongside the target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a "Negotiation Summary Sheet." Before listening, they review the categories: "Coach's Initial Suggestions," "Patient's Concerns/Counter-offers," "Agreed Habits/Technologies."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the full audio segment once to get the overall context of the negotiation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen a second time, specifically trying to fill in the "Negotiation Summary Sheet" with key details from each category. They should pay attention to how suggestions are given (e.g., "You might need to drink more water," "If you started tracking your sleep..."), and how ongoing habits are discussed (e.g., "I have been exercising...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups to compare their summary sheets and fill in any missing information they collectively remember from listening. They must justify their answers by referring to what they heard. This allows for collaborative listening and consolidation of understanding.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Directly simulates a real-life scenario of "Checking in on Health" through expert guidance and technology integration, demonstrating comprehension of complex interactions related to "Using Health Technology to Form Good Habits."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Demonstrating robust listening comprehension, identifying key points in an extended discussion, following a complex interactive dialogue, and recognizing specific language functions (suggestions, conditions, ongoing actions). This stage evaluates the student's ability to process and extract information from a negotiation context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of listening comprehension and self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group comparison (Stage 4) and collection of individual/group "Negotiation Summary Sheets." The teacher uses a simple checklist to note student ability to identify key information, grammatical structures, and vocabulary in the listening task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their completed "Negotiation Summary Sheets" from Stage 4. These sheets should accurately reflect the main suggestions, patient responses, and agreed action points from the health coach-patient negotiation audio.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying and summarizing the health coach's suggestions (modals, second conditional).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying the patient's ongoing habits or concerns (present perfect continuous).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (<i>tracker, app, well-being, mindfulness, hydration, routine</i>) in summaries.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of overall comprehension of the extended negotiation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of understanding the health coach's recommendations, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new health technology or habit mentioned today are you most interested in exploring further?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson, connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What listening strategies did you find most helpful today when trying to understand the different speakers and their health habits?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about identifying health suggestions and ongoing efforts in a conversation?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to enhance listening comprehension of abstract topics and negotiation scenarios? (e.g., "The progressive listening tasks were effective, and students responded well to the real-world scenario.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for future lessons, particularly for students struggling with B1.3 level listening? (e.g., "Some students struggled with the pace of the negotiation. For the next listening task, I could provide a partial transcript as a scaffold for lower-level learners or include more frequent pauses.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How will this lesson connect to the next lesson in the sequence? (e.g., "The vocabulary and concepts introduced and practiced in this listening lesson will be reinforced in Lesson 2, which will focus on reading comprehension of a text about using health technology to form good habits, expanding on the same themes and linguistic content.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>